---
import Legal from "../../layouts/Legal.astro"
import Fact from "../../components/Fact.astro"
import { sourceUrl } from "../../site"

const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ["agreement", "This agreement"],
  ["accounts", "Accounts and teams"],
  ["service", "The service"],
  ["your-content", "Your machines and your content"],
  ["rules", "Rules of use"],
  ["plans", "Plans, payment and renewal"],
  ["limits", "Limits and fair use"],
  ["suspension", "Suspension and termination"],
  ["data", "Your data, privacy and deletion"],
  ["open-source", "Open source and self-hosting"],
  ["warranty", "No warranty"],
  ["liability", "Limitation of liability"],
  ["indemnity", "Indemnity"],
  ["law", "Law and disputes"],
  ["changes", "Changes"],
  ["general", "General"],
  ["contact", "Contact"],
]
---

<Legal slug="terms" description="The agreement between you and Paperboat for the hosted service: accounts, plans, payment, fair use, suspension and liability." {sections}>
  <p slot="intro">
    These terms are the agreement between you and Paperboat (“Paperboat”, “we”, “us”) for using the hosted Paperboat service. Paperboat is
    operated by <Fact name="operators" /> until Paperboat, Inc., a Delaware C corporation, is formed (section 16). They are
    written to be read. Where a word matters legally, we say what we mean by it.
  </p>

  <section id="agreement">
    <h2>1. This agreement</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        By creating an account, connecting a machine, or paying for a plan, you agree to these terms, the
        <a href="/legal/acceptable-use/">Acceptable Use Policy</a> and the <a href="/legal/refunds/">Refund Policy</a>, which are part of them.
        The <a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> says how we handle personal data. For a team that needs it, the
        <a href="/legal/dpa/">Data Processing Addendum</a> also applies. These terms take effect on <Fact name="effective" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you use Paperboat for an organisation, for example with a work email or a team, you agree for that organisation and confirm you
        may do so. “You” then means both you and the organisation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="accounts">
    <h2>2. Accounts and teams</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>You must be at least 16 years old, and old enough to make a binding contract where you live to buy a paid plan.</li>
        <li>
          You sign in through our identity provider with an email address, a social login or your organisation's single sign-on. Keep the
          email current: it is where we send notices.
        </li>
        <li>
          You are responsible for everything done with your account, its machines, tokens and keys. Tell us at once if you think someone else
          has access. Removing a machine or revoking a token in the console stops it from connecting.
        </li>
        <li>
          One account is for one person. People who work together use a team. The team's owner decides who is a member, what each member can
          reach, and pays for the team's seats. Members' machines in a team can be reached by other members as the owner configures.
        </li>
        <li>
          You may not use Paperboat if you are subject to sanctions of the United Nations, the European Union, the United Kingdom, the United
          States or India, or from a place those sanctions cover, and you may not use it in breach of export or sanctions law. Breaking this
          is a material breach of these terms.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="service">
    <h2>3. The service</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Paperboat connects machines you control and gives you access to them: terminals, remote commands, SSH, file transfers, private access to
        ports, public previews and tunnels, encrypted environment variables (ENV), teams, and a web console. The hosted service is our control
        plane, our relays and our edges. The <code>pb</code> program runs on your machines.
      </p>
      <p>
        Traffic between your machines is end-to-end encrypted, and goes directly between them where the network allows or through a relay that
        forwards it without being able to read it. Public previews and tunnels are different: HTTPS ends at our edge, which reads each request
        to route it. <a href="/security/">Security</a> describes exactly who can read what.
      </p>
      <p>
        We work to keep the service available, but we do not promise a level of availability, and we may change, add or remove features. We
        tell you in advance by email or in the console when a change takes away something you pay for, and you may cancel if it does.
        Features marked as preview or beta are offered as they are and may change or end without notice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="your-content">
    <h2>4. Your machines and your content</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Everything on your machines, everything you send through Paperboat, and everything you publish stays yours. We claim no rights in it.
        You give us only the permission we need to run the service for you: to carry your traffic, to route and serve requests to your public
        links, to store the data the service needs (such as machine names, ENV ciphertext and audit events), and to act on abuse.
      </p>
      <p>
        You are responsible for what runs on your machines and what your previews and tunnels serve, for having the right to publish it, and
        for securing the applications you expose. A public link without access control can be opened by anyone with its address.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you send us feedback or ideas, we may use them without owing you anything. We do not use your content to train AI models.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <h2>5. Rules of use</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Follow the <a href="/legal/acceptable-use/">Acceptable Use Policy</a> and the law. In short: nothing illegal or harmful, no attacks on
        others or on us, no gaming the free plan, and no using previews and tunnels as a content-delivery network or file host. Do not get
        around plan limits, fair-use limits, rate limits, blocks or suspensions, and do not resell the hosted service without our written
        agreement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="plans">
    <h2>6. Plans, payment and renewal</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>The Free plan costs nothing. Paid plans, seats and extra bandwidth are priced on the <a href="/pricing/">pricing page</a> and in the console at the time you buy.</li>
        <li>
          Payment is taken by our payment processor, Stripe. Prices exclude taxes unless shown otherwise; we add the taxes the law requires.
          Receipts and invoices are in the console.
        </li>
        <li>
          Paid plans renew automatically, monthly or yearly, at the then-current price, until you cancel. Cancel any time in the console:
          cancelling stops the next renewal, and the plan keeps working until the end of the paid period. We give at least 30 days' notice by
          email of a price increase, and it applies from your next renewal.
        </li>
        <li>Seats added to a team are charged at once for the rest of the period. Seats removed stay paid until the next billing date.</li>
        <li>
          The service starts as soon as you pay. Payments are not refunded, as the <a href="/legal/refunds/">Refund Policy</a> describes,
          subject to the rights consumer law gives you there.
        </li>
        <li>
          If a payment fails, we tell you and retry. If it stays unpaid after the grace period on the pricing page, the workspace moves to the
          Free plan's limits.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>7. Limits and fair use</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each plan has limits on machines, previews and tunnels, terminals in the browser, and other resources, shown on the pricing page.
        Traffic through our relays and edges has a monthly fair-use amount at full speed and a slower rate after it, set out in the
        <a href="/legal/acceptable-use/#fair-use">Acceptable Use Policy</a>. Traffic directly between your machines, and through relays or
        edges you run yourself, is not limited by us.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="suspension">
    <h2>8. Suspension and termination</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <strong>By you.</strong> You can stop using Paperboat, cancel a plan, remove machines or delete your account at any time in the console.
        Deleting your account takes effect at once and cannot be undone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>By us.</strong> We may block a preview or tunnel, suspend an account or team, or close it, if you seriously or repeatedly break
        these terms or the Acceptable Use Policy, if your use puts other people, the service or us at risk, if a payment is disputed, or if the
        law requires it. Where we can, we warn you first and give you time to fix the problem; where the harm is serious or ongoing, we act first
        and tell you afterwards. The <a href="/legal/acceptable-use/#enforcement">Acceptable Use Policy</a> describes the steps and how to
        appeal.
      </p>
      <p>
        We may also close an account without a breach on your part, or stop offering the hosted service, with at least 30 days' notice. In that
        case we refund the unused part of any period you paid for in advance.
      </p>
      <p>
        When an account closes, access ends and we delete its data as described in the <a href="/legal/privacy/#retention">Privacy Policy</a>.
        Payments already due, and sections 4, 6, 9, 11, 12, 13 and 14, continue to apply.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>9. Your data, privacy and deletion</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        We handle personal data as the <a href="/legal/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> says. For data about other people that you or your team put
        into Paperboat, such as team members' accounts and audit events, we act as your processor under the
        <a href="/legal/dpa/">Data Processing Addendum</a>, which is part of these terms for every team and for any customer subject to data
        protection law that requires one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Back up what matters to you. Paperboat does not hold copies of what is on your machines. ENV values are encrypted with keys only you
        hold: if you lose your master password and your recovery code, we cannot recover them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="open-source">
    <h2>10. Open source and self-hosting</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Paperboat's source code is public at <a href={sourceUrl} rel="noopener">{sourceUrl.replace("https://", "")}</a>. Your use of the code
        itself, including running your own relay, edge or server, is governed by its licences (MIT for the client, relay and edge; AGPL-3.0 for
        the server and console), not by these terms. These terms govern the hosted service and your account. The Paperboat name and logo are
        ours; the licences do not grant rights to them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="warranty">
    <h2>11. No warranty</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows, the service is provided “as is” and “as available”, without warranties of any kind, express or implied,
        including that it is fit for a particular purpose, uninterrupted, error-free or secure against every attack. Nothing in these terms
        excludes a guarantee or right that consumer law gives you and that cannot be excluded.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="liability">
    <h2>12. Limitation of liability</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>To the extent the law allows:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Neither side is liable to the other for indirect or consequential loss, or for lost profits, revenue, data or goodwill, even if it was
          told the loss was possible.
        </li>
        <li>
          Each side's total liability arising from these terms or the service is limited to the greater of the amount you paid us in the 12
          months before the event that caused it, and 100 US dollars.
        </li>
        <li>
          These limits do not apply to your payment obligations, to your liability under section 13, or to liability that the law does not
          allow to be limited, such as for death or personal injury caused by negligence, for fraud, or for intent or gross negligence.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="indemnity">
    <h2>13. Indemnity</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        If someone makes a claim against us because of what you ran, sent or published through Paperboat, or because you broke these terms or
        the law, you will cover our reasonable costs and any amount we must pay as a result. We will tell you about the claim promptly and let
        you take part in the defence. This section does not apply to you as a consumer to the extent consumer law does not allow it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="law">
    <h2>14. Law and disputes</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        These terms are governed by the law of <Fact name="law" />, and disputes go to <Fact name="courts" />. If you are a consumer, you also
        keep the protection of the mandatory law of the country where you live, and may bring a claim in its courts. Please write to us first;
        most problems are solved by a conversation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <h2>15. Changes</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        We may change these terms. For a change that matters to you, we email you and show it in the console at least 30 days before it takes
        effect, unless the law or a security problem requires it sooner. If you do not agree, cancel and stop using the service before the
        change takes effect; a payment you already made for the current period is not affected. The date at the top shows the latest version.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="general">
    <h2>16. General</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>These terms, with the policies they name, are the whole agreement between you and us about the hosted service.</li>
        <li>If a part of them cannot be enforced, the rest still applies.</li>
        <li>If we do not enforce a right at once, we have not given it up.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Operator and successor.</strong> Paperboat is operated by <Fact name="operators" />, who are together the party to these
          terms, until Paperboat, Inc., a Delaware C corporation, is formed. When it is, these terms, the Data Processing Addendum, your account
          and any subscription transfer automatically to Paperboat, Inc., which takes on all our rights and obligations under them from that day.
          You agree to that transfer now; we tell you by email when it happens, and nothing else changes for you.
        </li>
        <li>You may not transfer these terms without our agreement. We may transfer them to a company that takes over the service, and will tell you.</li>
        <li>Neither side is responsible for a delay or failure caused by events outside its reasonable control.</li>
        <li>
          You agree to receive notices, receipts and other communications about the service electronically, by email to your account's
          address or in the console; they count as written. You send notices to the address in section 17.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <h2>17. Contact</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Paperboat, operated by <Fact name="operators" />. Postal address for legal notices: <Fact name="address" />. Email: <Fact name="legalEmail" />.</p>
      <p>Abuse: <a href="/report-abuse/">report it here</a>. Security vulnerabilities: see <a href="/security/#report">Security</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Legal>
